# Frontend (Vite/React mini-apps + Next.js) conventions

Each top-level app (`city-budget-simulator`, `council-accountability`,
`crossword-constructor`, `city-budget`, `time-timer`, `meeting-recap`,
`apps/problem-frame`) is an independent Vite (or Next.js) project with its own
`package.json`. Match the existing app's own idioms first; use these shared
rules to keep the apps consistent with each other where they don't conflict.

## Language & components

- New apps and new files in TypeScript-based apps use `.tsx`/`.ts`, function
  components, and typed props — no default `any`. `tsconfig.json` has `strict`,
  `noUnusedLocals`, and `noUnusedParameters` on; don't disable these per-file.
- Components live in `src/components/`, one component per file, named in
  PascalCase matching the file name (e.g. `StaffPanel.tsx` exports `StaffPanel`).
- Pure domain/simulation logic (no React) is separated out of components into
  its own directory (see `city-budget-simulator/src/simulation/`) so it can be
  unit tested independent of rendering.
- Path aliases (e.g. `@sim/*`) are defined in both `tsconfig.json` and
  `vite.config.ts` together — keep them in sync when adding one.

## State management

- Use `zustand` for shared/app-level state (see `src/store/gameStore.ts`); keep
  local component state in `useState`/`useReducer`. Don't introduce Redux,
  Context-as-a-store, or a second state library into an app that already has
  zustand.

## Styling

- Newer apps (crossword-constructor) use Tailwind v4 (`@tailwindcss/vite`
  or `@tailwindcss/postcss`) with utility classes in JSX; older apps use plain
  CSS files (`index.css`, `styles.css`). Follow whichever the app already uses
  rather than mixing approaches within one app.

## Testing

- Tests are colocated next to source (`Foo.test.ts` / `Foo.test.js` beside
  `Foo.ts`), run with `vitest run`. Prefer colocated tests over a separate
  `__tests__/` tree.
- Apps with React Testing Library (`crossword-constructor`) use
  `@testing-library/react` + `@testing-library/jest-dom` for component tests;
  pure logic modules get plain vitest unit tests without a DOM.

## Linting & config

- Each app has its own flat-config `eslint.config.{js,mjs,ts}` — extend it
  rather than adding a competing `.eslintrc`.
- Run `npm run lint` and `npm run build` (or `tsc -b`) before considering
  frontend changes done; `noUnusedLocals`/`noUnusedParameters` will fail the
  build on dead code.

## Next.js app (apps/problem-frame)

- Uses Next.js App Router, Drizzle ORM (`drizzle.config.ts`, `drizzle/`
  migrations), and `better-auth`. Schema changes go through
  `npm run db:generate` / `db:migrate`, never hand-edited SQL migrations.
- Tailwind v4 + `eslint-config-next`; keep server-only code (DB access, auth)
  out of client components.
